      "some_cephalopod_species_can_produce_so": "Some cephalopod species can produce SOUND by squirting water through their siphon at high speed — creating audible clicks. The function is unclear.",
      "some_deep_sea_cephalopods_discharge_bi": "Some deep-sea cephalopods discharge bioluminescent material as a decoy — instead of (or in addition to) the dark ink of shallow species. The glow distracts the predator while the animal escapes.",
      "some_deep_sea_cephalopods_magnapinna_s": "Some deep-sea cephalopods (Magnapinna squids) have arms that hang straight down + spread laterally, forming a giant \"umbrella\" — possibly for filter-feeding or prey detection.",
      "some_deep_sea_octopuses_are_translucen": "Some deep-sea octopuses are TRANSLUCENT — you can see their internal organs through their skin. Gigantopelta octopuses near hydrothermal vents are an example.",
      "some_deep_sea_octopuses_live_close_to_": "Some deep-sea octopuses live close to hydrothermal vents (Vulcanoctopus, Gigantopelta) — adapting to high-temperature high-sulfide chemistry.",
      "some_male_cuttlefish_are_sneakers_smal": "Some male cuttlefish are \"sneakers\" — small males that mimic female coloration to infiltrate harems guarded by larger males, then mate when the guard is distracted.",
      "some_octopus_species_exhibit_fishing_b": "Some octopus species exhibit \"fishing\" behavior — using their arms to lure small fish into a den where they can be ambushed.",
      "some_octopus_species_exhibit_mate_guar": "Some octopus species exhibit MATE GUARDING — males remain near females after mating to prevent other males from mating. This is rare in invertebrates.",
      "some_octopus_species_have_learned_to_u": "Some octopus species have learned to use sea urchins as protective umbrellas — coiling under the spines while resting.",
      "some_octopuses_ambush_prey_from_inside": "Some octopuses ambush prey from inside dens, only extending one arm to grab passers-by. They're patient — willing to wait many minutes.",
      "some_octopuses_appear_to_prefer_partic": "Some octopuses appear to PREFER particular caregivers — they emerge for one researcher but not another, even with the same approach behavior.",
      "some_octopuses_can_recognize_themselve": "Some octopuses can recognize themselves in a mirror if the mirror is small + tilted. The criteria for mirror self-recognition in cephalopods is contested.",
      "some_octopuses_can_zoom_through_small_": "Some octopuses can ZOOM through small gaps — fitting through holes the size of a quarter coin for 5-pound adults.",
      "some_octopuses_sleep_with_rem_like_ski": "Some octopuses sleep with REM-like skin color changes — possibly evidence of cephalopod dreaming.",
      "some_sensory_match_some_failure_mode_o": "Some sensory match, some failure mode. Outcome was uncertain.",
      "some_species_also_have_photoreceptors_": "Some species also have photoreceptors NEXT TO their photophores. They detect their own output + adjust it. This closed-loop control is what makes real counter-illumination so precise.",
      "some_species_turn_the_tables_a_50kg_gi": "Some species turn the tables. A 50kg giant Pacific octopus is documented eating small reef sharks (search \"Octopus eats shark\" — Bob Anderson 1992 footage). But the next year, a larger shark might eat THAT octopus. Predator/prey labels are size-relative + life-stage-relative.",
