      "sustainable_gardening_tips": "♻️ Sustainable Gardening Tips",
      "swollen_pink_red_nodules_on_the_roots_": "Swollen pink/red nodules on the roots contain billions of Rhizobium bacteria. These bacteria have an enzyme called nitrogenase that breaks the triple bond of atmospheric N₂ — one of the strongest chemical bonds in nature.",
      "tea_bags": "Tea bags",
      "the_companion_planting_of_corn_beans_a": "The companion planting of corn, beans, and squash — known as milpa in Mesoamerica — is one of humanity's oldest agricultural innovations. Archaeological evidence traces this system to over 7,000 years ago in present-day Mexico. Squash was domesticated ~10,000 years ago, maize ~9,000 years ago from the wild grass teosinte, and common beans ~7,000 years ago.",
      "the_edge_between_two_ecosystems_is_the": "The edge between two ecosystems is the most productive zone.",
      "the_fungus_extends_the_plant_s_root_sy": "The fungus extends the plant's root system by 100-1000×, accessing water and phosphorus the roots can't reach. In return, the plant shares 10-30% of its photosynthesized sugars. Both benefit — neither survives as well alone.",
      "the_golden_ratio_3_parts_brown_carbon_": "The golden ratio: 3 parts Brown (carbon) to 1 part Green (nitrogen). Keep moist like a wrung-out sponge!",
      "the_haudenosaunee_iroquois_confederacy": "The Haudenosaunee (Iroquois Confederacy) call these plants De-oh-há-ko — \"they sustain us.\" The Three Sisters are spiritual beings and precious gifts, central to Haudenosaunee language, ceremony, songs, and cosmology. This is not merely a farming technique — it is a living relationship between people and plants.",
      "the_milpa_system_diffused_northward_ov": "The milpa system diffused northward over millennia, appearing in North America around 1070 CE. By the time European colonizers arrived around 1500, it was a foundational food system across Central and North America.",
      "the_milpa_three_sisters_7_000_years_of": "The milpa / Three Sisters — 7,000+ years of agricultural science",
      "the_nitrogen_crisis": "The Nitrogen Crisis",
      "the_nitrogen_cycle": "♻️ The Nitrogen Cycle",
      "the_npk_cycle": "🧪 The NPK Cycle",
      "the_regenerative_garden": "The Regenerative Garden",
      "the_three_sisters": "The Three Sisters",
      "the_three_sisters_corn_beans_squash_is": "The Three Sisters (corn, beans, squash) is a 7,000-year-old agricultural system from Indigenous Mesoamerican and Haudenosaunee traditions. Plant them as neighbors and watch the synergy.",
      "the_three_sisters_de_oh_h_ko_meaning_t": "The Three Sisters (De-oh-há-ko, meaning “they sustain us” in Haudenosaunee) is a 7,000-year-old agricultural system originating in Mesoamerica. Indigenous agricultural science developed sophisticated polyculture techniques millennia before modern ecology.",
      "the_three_sisters_go_in_now_after_soil": "The Three Sisters go in NOW after soil reaches 60°F",
      "the_three_sisters_science_of_companion": "🌾 The Three Sisters: Science of Companion Planting",
      "the_wood_wide_web": "🍄 The Wood Wide Web",
      "these_invasive_insects_from_asia_suck_": "These invasive insects from Asia suck sap and excrete sticky honeydew that grows sooty mold.",
      "this_is_what_bean_roots_do_free_fertil": "🌱 THIS is what bean roots do! Free fertilizer from thin air!",
      "this_is_why_beans_are_so_important_the": "This is why beans are so important! They hack the nitrogen cycle through bacterial symbiosis.",
      "this_knowledge_is_not_historical_artif": "This knowledge is not historical artifact. Milpa and Three Sisters gardens are actively cultivated today across the Americas, representing a continuous tradition of ecological wisdom.",
      "this_reaction_converts_inert_nitrogen_": "This reaction converts inert nitrogen gas into ammonia (NH₃), which plant roots can absorb. It's the biological equivalent of the industrial Haber-Bosch process — but at room temperature, using only sunlight energy.",
      "this_single_equation_sustains_nearly_a": "This single equation sustains nearly all life on Earth. The glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) becomes the plant's energy and building material. The oxygen (O₂) is released — every breath you take was made by a plant.",
      "threats_to_the_network": "⚠️ Threats to the Network",
      "three_sisters": "Three Sisters",
      "three_sisters_2": "🌽 Three Sisters",
      "three_sisters_in_the_community": "Three Sisters in the Community",
      "three_sisters_synergy_discovery": "🌽 Three-sisters synergy discovery",
      "three_sisters_thrive": "🌽🫘🎃 Three Sisters thrive",
      "tilling_destroys_mycorrhizal_networks_": "Tilling destroys mycorrhizal networks (this is why no-till farming is gaining traction). Chemical fertilizers can suppress fungal growth — the plant gets \"lazy\" and stops feeding its fungal partner. Garlic mustard (an invasive) releases chemicals that specifically destroy mycorrhizal fungi, devastating the entire underground ecosystem.",
      "tiny_sap_sucking_insects_coat_the_stem": "Tiny sap-sucking insects coat the stems. They multiply explosively — one aphid can produce 80 offspring per week!",
      "together_corn_and_beans_provide_a_comp": "Together, corn and beans provide a complete protein — corn supplies methionine while beans supply lysine — forming the nutritional foundation of many Indigenous diets.",
